[ ] Give agent-facing verbs typed input and output\: show as JSON, text from stdin $NEEDS DECISION: which JSON shapes and which stdin/file flag convention should the agent-facing verbs use? The input half is decidable and ready (read note text from stdin or a file for add, update and jot); the output half is an agent-facing schema, which this project reserves for the user, and the repo does not pin it down. (a) show action --format json has exactly one canonical target: clearhead_core::to_schema_document, the specifications actions schema that read actions --format json already emits, so it conforms with no new schema. (b) show charter has NO canonical document schema -- specifications/schemas/charter_metadata.schema.json is the sidecar, not the charter -- and the repo already ships two inconsistent shapes: read charters --format json serializes MarkdownCharter with nested actions, while read actions --format json emits the canonical SchemaAction document. So show charter --format json must choose: reuse the shipped read charters object, serialize the domain Charter, or design a new canonical charter document shape. (c) The stdin/file flag needs a convention: existing file commands use the positional 'if not provided, reads from stdin' pattern, but a note is already a flag value, so the options are --note-file \[PATH|-] (optional value; omitted or - reads stdin), a sentinel on the existing flag (--description - , --append-description - , jot -), or a separate --description-file pair. Original finding follows unchanged. Found 2026-09-18. show action and show charter print tables only, so an agent cannot read an action back as data; and add or update take long notes as command-line arguments, so a note with backticks, dollar signs or brackets is fought through shell quoting and the grammar. Add a JSON output form to show, and let add, update and jot read the note from stdin or a file flag. This is the CLI half of the typed-input problem and benefits any harness with a shell. Related: the append-description and newline actions; do them as one pass over the note path.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:39 #01a0b5d0-da47-714b-9a4e-113403989cea
